{"name":"Crease","hex":"#7a6d44","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crease","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A line or mark made by folding or doubling any pliable substance; hence, a similar mark, however produced.","One of the white lines drawn on the pitch to show different areas of play; especially the popping crease, but also the bowling crease and the return crease."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a crease in; to wrinkle.","To undergo creasing; to form wrinkles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crease"}],"entries":[{"title":"Line (geometry)","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a straight line, usually abbreviated line, is an infinitely long object with no width, depth, or curvature. It is a special case of a curve and an idealisation of such physical objects as a straightedge, a taut string, or a ray of light. Lines are spaces of dimension one, which may be embedded in spaces of dimension two, three, or higher. The word line may also refer, in everyday life, to a line segment, which is a part of a line delimited by two points."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Gerade.svg/330px-Gerade.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Line_(geometry)"},{"title":"Crease (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Crease is an American hard rock band that formed in Ft. Lauderdale, Florida in 1994."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Crease_Live-1.jpg/330px-Crease_Live-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crease_(band)"},{"title":"Crease (cricket)","blocks":[{"text":"In cricket, a crease is a white line painted or chalked on the field of play, that defines the area within which the batters and bowlers operate. The term crease may also be used to refer to the rectangular area enclosed by the lines. Law 7 of the Laws of Cricket governs the size and position of the crease markings, and defines the actual line as the back edge of the width of the marked line on the soil, i.e., the edge nearest to the wicket at that end."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/North_London_Cricket_Club_wicket_and_stumps_Crouch_End_London_England_1.jpg/330px-North_London_Cricket_Club_wicket_and_stumps_Crouch_End_London_England_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crease_(cricket)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Great_circle_hemispheres.png/500px-Great_circle_hemispheres.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Great_circle_hemispheres.png/500px-Great_circle_hemispheres.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A great circle divides the sphere in two equal hemispheres, while also satisfying the \"no curvature\" property.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreat_circle_hemispheres.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Crease_Live-2.jpg/330px-Crease_Live-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Crease_Live-2.jpg/330px-Crease_Live-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crease at Zetafest 1999","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrease_Live-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Crease_OH2004.jpg/330px-Crease_OH2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Crease_OH2004.jpg/330px-Crease_OH2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crease in 